The namespace argument, \code{ns}, is passed on to \code{\link[XML:getNodeSet]{XML::getNodeSet()}} or
\code{\link[xml2:xml_find_all]{xml2::xml_find_all()}} if it is necessary to use a namespace present
within the document. It can be ignored for content lacking a namespace.
For \pkg{xml2}, leaving it at \code{NULL} means the namespaces the document
declares are collected, with \code{\link[xml2:xml_ns]{xml2::xml_ns()}}, for every query; passing a
zero-length \code{ns} skips that walk of the document, which is worth doing in
a loop over a large document known to be un-namespaced.

A document with a \emph{default} namespace — one declared as
\code{xmlns="..."}, as XHTML, SVG and Atom documents are — needs its
element names prefixed in the selector even though the markup does not
prefix them: XPath 1.0 has no notion of a default namespace, so an
unprefixed name only matches elements in no namespace at all, and a
bare \code{"p"} finds nothing. With \pkg{xml2} the default \code{ns = NULL}
collects the document's namespaces with \code{\link[xml2:xml_ns]{xml2::xml_ns()}}, which names
each default namespace \code{d1}, \code{d2}, and so on, so \code{"d1|p"} selects the
\code{p} elements. \code{"*|p"} selects them whatever the prefix and works with
\pkg{XML} too, as does naming the namespace yourself
(\code{ns = c(x = "http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml")}, then \code{"x|p"}). A
document read as HTML, by \code{\link[XML:htmlParse]{XML::htmlParse()}} or \code{\link[xml2:read_html]{xml2::read_html()}},
is not affected: libxml2's HTML parser puts elements in no namespace,
so bare names match. The rule reaches inside \verb{:is()}, \verb{:where()},
\verb{:not()}, \verb{:has()} and \verb{of S} as well, so \code{":is(p)"} finds nothing
wherever \code{"p"} does; prefix the names there too.

Selectors are translated with the \code{generic} (XML) translator unless a
\code{translator} argument is given to be passed on to \code{\link[=css_to_xpath]{css_to_xpath()}},
with one exception: a document parsed as HTML, by \code{\link[XML:htmlParse]{XML::htmlParse()}} or
\code{\link[xml2:read_html]{xml2::read_html()}}, is queried with the \code{html} translator, so that
element and attribute names are matched case-insensitively and the
